# SSH tunnel list. Copy to tunnels.conf (per machine, not synced) and edit.
# Saving tunnels.conf applies changes automatically via tunnels-sync.path.
#
# Format (whitespace-separated, one tunnel per line):
#   name  ssh_target  remote_port  [local_port]  [remote_host]
#
#   name         Label only, no network meaning: becomes tunnel@<name>.service.
#                Letters, digits, - and _. Renaming a line restarts that tunnel.
#   ssh_target   Anything `ssh` accepts: user@ip, user@hostname, or a ~/.ssh/config alias.
#   remote_port  Port on the remote side.
#   local_port   Port opened on 127.0.0.1 here. Omit or use - to reuse remote_port.
#   remote_host  Host the remote side connects to. Default 127.0.0.1 (the SSH server itself).
#
# Each line runs as its own ssh process, restarted by systemd 10s after it exits:
#   ssh -N -L 127.0.0.1:<local_port>:<remote_host>:<remote_port> <ssh_target>
# e.g. "a6000 james@192.0.2.10 8080" forwards localhost:8080 to port 8080 on 192.0.2.10.
#
# Rules:
#   - Names and local ports must be unique; local ports must be >= 1024.
#   - Any invalid line rejects the whole file and leaves running tunnels unchanged.
#   - Before adding a new host or IP, run `ssh <ssh_target>` once by hand to accept
#     its host key. Tunnels use key auth only and cannot answer prompts.
#
# Check:  systemctl --user status tunnel@<name>
# Logs:   journalctl --user -u tunnel@<name> -f
#         journalctl --user -u tunnels-sync -n 20   (rejected config reasons)
#
# Examples (uncomment to use):

# Inference server; local port defaults to the same number as the remote port.
#a6000        james@192.0.2.10     8080

# Same service on a second server; remote 8080 is taken locally, so map it to 8081.
#a6000-b      mark@192.0.2.11      8080   8081

# Host defined in ~/.ssh/config.
#lab-jupyter  lab-server           8888

# Database on a private host reachable only from the SSH server.
#db-private   james@192.0.2.10     5432   15432   10.0.0.20
